{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target describes a discriminating simulation in prose ('A sandboxed simulation comparing naive DFS against symmetry-pruned DFS will measure the node expansion count to reach 45 comparators') but provides simulation_refs: []. The problem context explicitly states that 'a simulation described in prose with an empty simulation_refs channel is an unverified claim and will be criticized as such.' The target files no typed sandboxed Python program, so its central rival prediction — that symmetry-pruned DFS expands fewer nodes than naive DFS — is unverified and cannot discriminate any structural hypothesis. The counterconditions themselves are labeled 'observation' but no observation channel was filed to resolve them.","target":"24c9294f73e6504e2bcc90fb558bf53ce6f8a8dae70f9ec7287abf88397a2d46"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target's causal mechanism rests on a TSP-to-sorting-network analogy, but the disanalogies it itself enumerates undercut the mechanism without a bridging account. Simulated annealing requires a graded energy function so that the cooling schedule can accept 'worse' solutions with nonzero probability that decreases over time. The 0-1 principle yields a binary pass/fail signal — a candidate network either sorts all 8192 binary vectors or it does not — so a rewired or comparator-stripped network almost certainly fails the check, producing a flat (zero-gradient) landscape with no informative signal to guide the annealing walk. The target lists 'TSP solutions form a continuous distance landscape, while sorting networks have a discrete validation landscape' as a disanalogy but never specifies the energy function that would make SA operational on this discrete, all-or-nothing validation. Without such a function, the claim that SA will 'reliably find 45-comparator networks' lacks a causal mechanism: the shared structure of 'accept worse solutions temporarily' cannot function when there is no graded notion of 'worse' to accept.","